# Copy to .env (gitignored) and fill in your own values.
#
# These are BUILD-TIME variables: Vite inlines anything prefixed with VITE_ into
# the bundle, so they are visible in the shipped JavaScript — never put a secret
# here (that's why the session and accounts live on the server instead).
#
# Both are optional. Without them the app falls back to the keys this project
# shipped with, so a plain `npm run build` still works.
#
# TMDB key: https://www.themoviedb.org/settings/api
VITE_TMDB_KEY=
#
# Embed player API key (NexStream): https://api.codespecters.com/api
# Optional: the NexStream chip is always listed last (the keyless embed servers
# keep the primary slot), but without this key those URLs will not play. Whatever
# is set here is visible in the shipped bundle.
VITE_EMBED_API_KEY=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# SERVER-side variables
#
# Read at startup: from the real process environment first, and for anything not
# already set there, from this file (see server/env-auto.js). On AlwaysData the
# panel — Web > Sites > your site > Environment — therefore always wins, so you
# can set a value in either place. All are optional.
#
# The file lives outside dist/, so the static handler can never serve it.
# WAMPYSU_ENV_FILE=off disables reading it entirely.
#
# TMDB key used by the server for title-page <meta> tags and sitemap.xml.
# No default: unset means no server-side TMDB calls — the sitemap lists only
# the two libraries and title pages keep the shell's generic tags. Set it in
# the AlwaysData panel (Web > Sites > Environment) rather than here in prod.
# TMDB_API_KEY=
# Override the TMDB host (the test suite points this at a fake server).
# TMDB_API_BASE=https://api.themoviedb.org
# How long a title page waits for its meta tags before using generic ones.
# TMDB_META_TIMEOUT_MS=4000
# In-memory cache lifetimes for title details and the sitemap's title list.
# TMDB_META_TTL_MS=21600000
# TMDB_LIST_TTL_MS=21600000
# Cap on title URLs listed in sitemap.xml (0 disables them).
# SITEMAP_MAX_TITLES=500
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sessions and the Android app's personalised feed.
#
# How long a session lasts. SESSION_DAYS is the default; a sign-in that ticks
# "Keep me logged in" (the switch on the app's onboarding and sign-in screens)
# gets SESSION_KEEP_DAYS instead.
# SESSION_DAYS=30
# SESSION_KEEP_DAYS=180
#
# The feed is built from the account's own watch history: genre affinity is
# counted across the most recent titles and used for a TMDB discover query.
# These knobs are optional tuning; the defaults suit a small library.
# How many recent titles are inspected for genre affinity.
# FEED_SEED_LIMIT=5
# Cap on the rows one feed response may carry.
# FEED_MAX_ROWS=12
# How long the feed's TMDB lookups are cached in memory.
# FEED_CACHE_TTL_MS=21600000
# How long one feed TMDB call may take before that row is simply skipped.
# FEED_TMDB_TIMEOUT_MS=4500
#
# The /healthz switch, and the secret that guards it. Unset (the default) the
# probe answers `{ ok, uptimeSec }` and nothing else, which is all a liveness
# check needs and all that is safe on a public endpoint. Set it and the probe
# returns the operator diagnostics instead — but only to a caller presenting
# x-health-token: <value>, or ?token=<value> for monitors that cannot send
# headers; everyone else gets 401. There is no half-open state: set it and every
# monitor has to send it.
# HEALTH_TOKEN=
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the app writes: the media root (see README, Storage).
#
# <media>/accounts/ holds the account store and one folder per account;
# <media>/tracing/ holds the sign-in trace. The default is ~/media/lumiere, and
# falls back to ./data if that directory cannot be created.
#
# ON ALWAYSDATA SET THIS, or your accounts land somewhere you did not choose.
# LUMIERE_MEDIA_DIR=/home/lumiere/lumiere
# Each half can be moved on its own — e.g. keep the trace on another volume.
# LUMIERE_ACCOUNTS_DIR=/home/lumiere/lumiere/accounts
# LUMIERE_TRACING_DIR=/home/lumiere/lumiere/tracing
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The sign-in trace: one JSON line per sign-in and sign-up — the address, the
# place, the network, and whether it looked like a VPN, proxy or Tor exit. It is
# written off the request, never awaited, and the queue is bounded.
#
# Providers are tried in order and the first that answers wins. Each has a per-day
# ceiling so a small free tier is skipped rather than answering errors until the
# month turns over. ip-api.com needs no key at all and is what makes this work on
# a fresh deployment — but its terms are NON-COMMERCIAL ONLY, so a commercial
# deployment should set GEO_DISABLE_IPAPI=1.
# https://ipstack.com (geo + network + security)  https://positionstack.com (address)
# IPSTACK_API_KEY=
# POSITIONSTACK_API_KEY=
# VPNAPI_KEY=            # https://vpnapi.io — security + geo, ~1000/month
# PROXYCHECK_KEY=        # https://proxycheck.io — VPN/proxy/Tor flags
# The free ipstack and positionstack plans are HTTP-only; a paid plan can use TLS.
# IPSTACK_URL=http://api.ipstack.com
# POSITIONSTACK_URL=http://api.positionstack.com/v1/reverse
# Daily ceilings per provider (0 disables that provider). The ipstack default is
# tiny on purpose: its free plan is ~100 lookups a month, so it answers the first
# few sign-ins of the day and the keyless fallback does the rest.
# IPSTACK_MAX_PER_DAY=3
# POSITIONSTACK_MAX_PER_DAY=500
# VPNAPI_MAX_PER_DAY=30
# PROXYCHECK_MAX_PER_DAY=100
# IPAPI_MAX_PER_DAY=900
# GEO_TIMEOUT_MS=4000       # per lookup
# GEO_CACHE_MINUTES=360     # how long one address's answer is reused
#
# Refusing VPNs is OPT-IN: a false positive locks a real customer out. The
# default records the flag and lets the visitor in.
# VPN_BLOCK_MODE=flag       # flag | block
# VPN_BLOCK_MESSAGE=VPN and proxy connections cannot sign in to Lumiere. Turn off your VPN and try again.
# VPN_FLAG_HOSTING=         # 1 to count datacenter/hosting ranges as VPNs too
#
# TRACING=0 switches the whole thing off (no lookup, no file).
# TRACE_KEEP_DAYS=180       # how long the daily files are kept
# TRACE_QUEUE_MAX=500
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# At-rest encryption for the user data stores (accounts, sessions, resets,
# watch history). On by default: emails become HMAC pseudonyms and record
# bodies become AES-256-GCM ciphertext (see server/crypt.js). The master key
# is read from WAMPYSU_MASTER_KEY when set — the environment always wins, so
# the AlwaysData panel is the right place for it — and otherwise generated
# once into data/.wampysu-key (chmod 600). BACK THAT FILE UP: losing the key
# makes every encrypted store permanently unreadable. Set to 0 to store
# everything in the old plaintext shapes.
# WAMPYSU_MASTER_KEY=
# WAMPYSU_ENCRYPT=1
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profile photos (uploaded from My Home). Stored as real files, not JSON, so
# they can be served directly and mirrored off-box with rclone.
#
# By default each photo lives in its account's own folder —
# <media>/accounts/<id>/avatar.png|jpg — next to that account's record, so an
# account's data is one directory. Setting AVATAR_DIR switches to the older flat
# layout (one directory, files named by pseudonym), which is what the rclone
# mirror in docs/avatar-storage.md is written against. Set one or the other, and
# migrate any existing files when you change it.
# Only JPEG and PNG are ever accepted, whatever the client claims.
# AVATAR_DIR=/home/lumiere/lumiere/avatars
#
# The app's prefs.json (My List, likes, saved titles, devices) and the at-rest
# master key stay here, whatever the media root is set to. The key file is never
# moved: a new path means a new key, and a new key makes every stored account
# unreadable.
# WAMPYSU_DATA_DIR=./data
# MAX_AVATAR_BYTES=2097152
# AVATAR_UPLOADS_PER_IP_HOUR=40
# AVATAR_UPLOADS_PER_SITE_HOUR=400
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Outgoing mail — the welcome email to a new account, and password-reset links.
#
# Set these in AlwaysData under Web > Sites > your site > Environment.
# AlwaysData's mail server is smtp-<account>.alwaysdata.net: port 465 is
# implicit TLS, 587 is STARTTLS (SMTP_SECURE is worked out from the port).
# The login is the full email address and its mailbox password.
#
# Nothing is sent — and the boot log says so — until SMTP_HOST and SMTP_FROM
# are both set, so the app is safe to deploy before the mailbox exists.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp-lumiere.alwaysdata.net
# SMTP_PORT=465
# SMTP_USER=lumiere@alwaysdata.net
# SMTP_PASS=
# SMTP_FROM=lumiere@alwaysdata.net
# SMTP_FROM_NAME=Lumiere
# How long a reset link stays valid, and the gap between reset mails to one
# address. Both are also the knobs that limit how often mail can be sent.
# RESET_TTL_MINUTES=60
# RESET_COOLDOWN_SECONDS=60
#
# Verification codes are the other kind of mail this app sends: the six digits
# behind every change on the User Settings screen (email, password, Google,
# gender/date of birth, deletion). A code lives for VERIFY_TTL_MINUTES, is
# single-use, and is thrown away after VERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS wrong guesses — which
# is what makes a six-digit code unguessable. The request throttles sit per
# account and per visitor. Without SMTP the settings screen says so and offers
# the password reset email instead.
# VERIFY_TTL_MINUTES=10
# VERIFY_MAX_ATTEMPTS=5
# VERIFY_CODES_PER_EMAIL_HOUR=10
# VERIFY_CODES_PER_IP_HOUR=30
# EMAIL_CHANGES_PER_IP_HOUR=10
# ACCOUNT_DELETES_PER_IP_HOUR=5
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CAPTCHA on the sign-in and sign-up forms (optional).
#
# Google reCAPTCHA v2 (the "I'm not a robot" checkbox). Create the keys at
# https://www.google.com/recaptcha/admin/create — pick reCAPTCHA v2 ▸ "I'm not
# a robot" Checkbox — and add every hostname this build is served from
# (lumiere.alwaysdata.net, plus localhost for `npm run dev`) under Domains, or
# the widget will refuse to render. Until BOTH values are set, no widget is
# shown and nothing is enforced — the forms behave exactly as they did before.
# The site key is public (it ships in the page); the secret stays on the server.
#
# The token is verified on signup, on login, and again before the Google flow
# leaves for Google, so neither a typed password nor the Google button can skip
# it. A failed or missing token always answers the same sentence: "Please
# verify with CAPTCHA."
# RECAPTCHA_SITE_KEY=
# RECAPTCHA_SECRET=
# Override the siteverify host (the test suite points this at a fake server).
# RECAPTCHA_VERIFY_URL=https://www.google.com/recaptcha/api/siteverify
# RECAPTCHA_TIMEOUT_MS=8000
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Sign in with Google (optional).
#
# Both values come from https://console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services →
# Credentials → Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application. The
# client secret is a real secret: it belongs in this file (or the AlwaysData
# panel) and never in the source tree or the client bundle.
#
# Under "Authorised redirect URIs" add the callback path on every origin the
# app is served from, exactly — Google compares it byte for byte:
#   https://lumiere.alwaysdata.net/api/auth/google/callback
#   http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback      (for `npm run dev`)
#
# The redirect URI is derived from the origin the visitor is actually using,
# so PUBLIC_ORIGIN is the knob to reach for when the app is behind a proxy that
# forwards an unexpected Host. Until both variables are set, no Google button
# is shown and the routes answer that it is not configured — the app behaves
# exactly as it did before.
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://lumiere.alwaysdata.net
